use std
into playout.amni

This file is a module with no main (namespace playout). It turns the glyphs `pcontent` records for each page into reading-ordered paragraphs, list items, headings and tables, removes running headers and footers, and writes the result into the `dm` document model. It also has the whole-PDF entry point `pl_read_pdf`. Every module-level name starts with `pl_` or `PL_`. Sizes are in PDF points; "s" in a rule means the larger font size of the two things compared. PDF objects are read only through these `pdf` functions, which all take node indexes and dereference them: `pdf::pf_kind(n)` (a `pdf::PF_` type constant such as `pdf::PF_DICT`, `pdf::PF_STREAM`, `pdf::PF_ARR`, `pdf::PF_NAME`, `pdf::PF_INT`, `pdf::PF_REAL`, or -1), `pdf::pf_dget(n, key)` (the value node of a dict or stream for key, a name written without its slash, or -1), `pdf::pf_dhas(n, key)`, `pdf::pf_dict_of(n)`, `pdf::pf_bytes(n)` (the bytes of a name or string node), `pdf::pf_name_is(n, v)`, `pdf::pf_int(n, default)`, `pdf::pf_real(n, default)`, `pdf::pf_len(n)` and `pdf::pf_at(n, i)` (array length and element), `pdf::pf_decode(n, out) bool` (a stream's decoded data) and `pdf::pf_text(n, out)` (a text string as UTF-8); `pdf::pf_get(num)` takes an object number, not a node, and is not needed here.

```amni
import math;
import pdf;
import dcrypt;
import pfont;
import pcontent;
import dm;
```

`struct PlSpan` has `u0 real`, `u1 real`, `v real`, `size real`, `at int`, `len int` (UTF-8 text in `pl_txt`), `nb int` (bold glyph count), `n int` (glyph count), `line int`, `used bool`, `blk int`, `w0 int`, `wn int` (its words: entries w0 to w0 + wn − 1 of `pl_words`).

`struct PlWord` has `u0 real`, `u1 real`, `at int`, `len int` (the word's UTF-8 text in `pl_txt`, without spaces).

`struct PlLine` has `u0 real`, `u1 real`, `v real`, `size real`, `at int`, `len int`, `bold bool`.

`struct PlBox` has `u0 real`, `u1 real`, `top real`, `bot real`, `first int`, `count int` (its output records in `pl_out`), `table bool`.

`struct PlOut` has `page int`, `kind int` (`dm::DM_PARA`, `dm::DM_ITEM`, `dm::DM_ROW`, 100 for a table start, 101 for a table end), `level int`, `at int`, `len int` (text in `pl_otxt`), `size real`, `bold bool`, `lines int`, `ftop real`, `fbot real` (the record's top and bottom as fractions of the page height measured from the top, 0.5 for text that is not upright), `drop bool`.

Module state: `var pl_spans PlSpan[>..]`, `var pl_txt byte[>..]`, `var pl_words PlWord[>..]`, `var pl_lines PlLine[>..]`, `var pl_ltxt byte[>..]`, `var pl_boxes PlBox[>..]`, `var pl_tmp_out PlOut[>..]` (the current page group's records before ordering), `var pl_out PlOut[>..]`, `var pl_otxt byte[>..]`, `var pl_parent int[>..]`, `var pl_idx int[>..]`, `var pl_tmp byte[>..]`, `var pl_tmp2 byte[>..]`, `var pl_scanned = 0`, `var pl_pages = 0`, `var pl_dir = 0`, `var pl_width = 1.0`, `var pl_height = 1.0`.

`pl_frame(x real, y real, u real&, v real&)` maps an upright page point into the reading frame of direction `pl_dir`: 0 gives u = x, v = y; 1 gives u = y, v = −x; 2 gives u = −x, v = −y; 3 gives u = −y, v = x. In every frame text runs toward larger u and earlier lines have larger v.

`pl_sort(idx int[>..]&, key1 const real[:], key2 const real[:])` stable-sorts the index list by key1 descending and then key2 ascending (a merge sort; keys are indexed by the values in idx).

`pl_ends_space(b const byte[:]) bool` and `pl_starts_space(b const byte[:]) bool` test for a space byte at the end or start.

`pl_build_spans()` clears `pl_spans` and `pl_txt` and walks the glyphs of `pcontent::pc_g` in order, taking only those whose dir is `pl_dir`. Each glyph's frame values are u0 and v from its origin and u1 from its end (u1 = u0 when the end comes out smaller). A glyph is dropped as a duplicate when one of the last four glyphs taken has the same text and lies within 0.15 × size of it in both u and v (fake bold and shadows). A glyph continues the open span when |v − span v| ≤ 0.35 s and u0 ≥ span u1 − 0.6 s and u0 − span u1 ≤ 1.0 s; otherwise the open span is closed and the glyph starts a new one. Every rule here uses the glyph's text with the Latin ligatures expanded: U+FB00 becomes "ff", U+FB01 "fi", U+FB02 "fl", U+FB03 "ffi", U+FB04 "ffl", U+FB05 and U+FB06 "st" (other text is kept as is). A space glyph (text that is only spaces) never starts a span and never changes u1: inside an open span it records its u0 as the span's pending space position, unless one is already pending. Before appending a non-space glyph to a span, append one space when a space position is pending and the glyph's u0 minus that position is at least 0.1 × its size (a space squeezed to nothing by negative word spacing joins the word instead), or when u0 − span u1 > 0.13 s, unless the span text already ends with a space or the glyph text starts with one; then clear the pending space position. A new span starts with no pending space. Appending a glyph adds its text, sets u1 to the larger of both, size to the larger, increases n and, when `pfont::fo_is_bold` of the glyph's font, nb. Words: clear `pl_words` together with `pl_spans`; a span's words are appended to `pl_words` while it is built, so they are contiguous (set w0 when the span starts). A non-space glyph starts a new word (u0 and u1 from the glyph, at = the position in `pl_txt` where its text goes, len 0) when it is the span's first glyph, when the span text ends with a space at the moment it is appended (after the space rule above), or when its own text starts with a space (then the word begins after those leading spaces); otherwise it extends the span's last word, setting that word's u1 to the larger of both. Either way the word's len grows by the glyph text's length (not counting leading or trailing spaces of the glyph text). A closed span is kept only when its text has a byte other than space (a dropped span's words are removed from the end of `pl_words`); trailing spaces are trimmed.

`pl_assign_lines()` sorts the span indices by v descending then u0 (`pl_sort`), then walks them: a span starts a new line id when its v differs from the v of the current line's first span by more than 0.35 × the larger size. Then it rebuilds `pl_spans` in (line id, u0) order and merges each span into the previous one in the same line when the gap (next u0 minus previous u1) lies between −0.3 s and 1.0 s, adding a space between them when the gap is above 0.13 s and neither side already has one (merging concatenates the text into a fresh `pl_txt` entry and combines u ranges, sizes and counts; it also appends fresh copies of the previous span's words followed by the next span's words to the end of `pl_words` and points w0 and wn at them, each copied word's at moved by the same amount its text moved inside the fresh text entry).

`pl_prose(sp int) bool` is true when span sp has at least 25 text bytes and is wider than 0.22 × `pl_width`.

`pl_tables()` finds tables among the lines of `pl_spans` (spans sharing a line id, in u order). A line qualifies as a table row when it has at least 2 spans and at most one of them is prose (`pl_prose`). A table is a run of consecutive lines in which the distance between neighbouring line baselines is at most 3.0 × the run's largest span size, every line qualifies except that a single-span line may appear after the first row as a continuation line when its span is not prose, its size is within 15 percent of the previous line's largest span size, it is mostly bold (nb × 2 > n) exactly when most spans of the previous line are, and its baseline lies less than 1.6 × the previous line's largest span size below the previous line's baseline; any other line ends the run. A table needs at least 3 qualifying rows, or at least 2 when each of them has 3 or more spans. Columns come from the words of the qualifying rows only (continuation lines never shape columns): sort those words by u0 and sweep them into segments, a word joining the current segment when its u0 ≤ segment u1 + 0.1 × its span's size (extending u1 to the larger), otherwise starting a new segment. Then, from left to right, merge a segment into the one before it when at least one qualifying row has words in both and, for every qualifying row that has words in both, the distance from the end (u1) of its last word in the left segment to the start (u0) of its first word in the right segment is below 1.0 × that row's largest span size, because that is a word space inside a cell and not a gap between columns; after a merge, test the merged segment against the next one the same way. The segments left are the columns: a table needs at least 2 columns and every qualifying row must have words in at least 2 of them, otherwise the run is not a table (try again from the next line). Cells: every word of a qualifying row goes to the column whose extent contains its u0 (the nearest column when none does), the words of one cell joined with single spaces in u order; each word of a continuation line appends, after a space, to the previous row's cell of the column its u0 falls in (the nearest column when none). For each table add a box with `table` true and its bounds (top = highest baseline + its size, bottom = lowest baseline − 0.3 × its size), and records into `pl_tmp_out`: a kind 100 record, then one `dm::DM_ROW` record per row with the cells joined by tab bytes (empty cells stay empty, every row has one cell per column), then a kind 101 record; the box's first and count cover those records. Mark the spans used.

`pl_find(i int) int` and `pl_union(a int, b int)` are union-find over `pl_parent` with path halving.

`pl_blocks()` groups the unused spans into blocks: for each unused span a and each later unused span b (in line order, stopping once b's baseline is more than 2.0 × the larger size below a's), join them when they share a line id and the gap between their u ranges is below 1.2 s, or when a's baseline is above b's by more than 0.35 s and at most 1.75 s, their u ranges overlap by more than 0.5, the larger size is at most 1.3 times the smaller, and both or neither are mostly bold (nb × 2 > n). Each block's spans are then turned into lines and records by `pl_block_paragraphs`.

`pl_item_start(t const byte[:], strip int&) bool` is true when a line starts a list item: the text begins with one of • ◦ ▪ ‣ ● ○ ■ □ – — - * · (followed by a space, tab or nothing else), in which case strip is the byte length of the bullet and the spaces after it; or with 1 to 3 digits, a single letter, or 1 to 5 of the letters ivxlcdm (either case), followed by "." or ")" and a space, or the same wrapped in parentheses followed by a space, in which case strip is 0.

`pl_join_line(out byte[>..]&, line const byte[:])` appends a line to a paragraph: when out is empty just append; when out ends with "-" or U+00AD preceded by a lower-case letter and the line starts with a lower-case letter, remove that hyphen and append directly; otherwise append a space and the line.

`pl_block_paragraphs(members const int[:])` takes one block's span indices: group them into lines by line id (spans in u order, joined with a space when the gap is above 0.13 s, nothing otherwise); the line's u0 and u1 are its first and last span's, size the largest, bold when every span is mostly bold. The block's left edge is the smallest line u0 and its right edge the largest u1; its line spacing is the median baseline distance between consecutive lines (when there are at least 3 lines; otherwise 1.2 × the largest size). Start a new paragraph before a line when: it starts a list item (`pl_item_start`); its boldness differs from the previous line's; its size differs from the previous line's by more than 15 percent; its baseline is more than 1.5 × the line spacing below the previous one; it starts more than 0.8 × size right of the left edge while the previous line started within 0.5 × size of it and the current paragraph is not a list item; or the previous line ends more than 4 × size before the right edge and its text ends with . ! ? : ” " or ). Lines of a paragraph are joined with `pl_join_line`. Each paragraph becomes a record in `pl_tmp_out`: kind `dm::DM_ITEM` when its first line starts an item (the bullet stripped per `pl_item_start`; level 1 plus the rounded (line u0 minus the smallest item u0 in the block) / (1.5 × size), at most 4), otherwise `dm::DM_PARA`; size is the largest line size, bold when all lines are bold, lines the line count. ftop and fbot come from the paragraph's highest baseline + size and lowest baseline − 0.25 × size: for direction 0 they are (`pl_height` − value) / `pl_height` clamped to 0..1, otherwise 0.5. The block becomes a box with its bounds and its records.

`pl_order()` orders the boxes of the current group and appends their records to `pl_out` in that order. With more than 200 boxes order them by top descending then u0 ascending. Otherwise build edges: a before b when their u ranges overlap by more than 0.5 and a's vertical middle is above b's; or when a lies left of b (a u1 < b u0 + 1) and no third box c whose vertical middle lies strictly between a's and b's overlaps both of them in u. Take boxes in topological order, always choosing among boxes with no unplaced predecessor the one with the highest top (then the smallest u0); when none is free (a cycle), take the unplaced box with the highest top.

`pl_layout_page(p int)` lays out page p from `pcontent`: set `pl_width` and `pl_height` from `pcontent::pc_pw` and `pcontent::pc_ph` (swapped for directions 1 and 3); for directions 0, 3, 1, 2 in that order: set `pl_dir`, clear the boxes and `pl_tmp_out`, then `pl_build_spans`, `pl_assign_lines`, `pl_tables`, `pl_blocks`, `pl_order`; every record gets page p.

`pl_norm_key(t const byte[:]) int` is an FNV-1a 32-bit hash of t lower-cased with every ASCII digit replaced by "#" and spaces removed.

`pl_page_number_like(t const byte[:]) bool` is true for text that is only a page number: digits; roman numerals of up to 7 letters; "page" (any case) followed by a number and optionally "of" and a number; a number, "/" or "of", and a number; or a number between dashes.

`pl_finish()` decides headings and margins and writes `pl_out` into `dm`:
- Body size: over all text records (kinds `dm::DM_PARA` and `dm::DM_ITEM`), the size rounded to the nearest 0.5 with the largest total text length.
- Margins: a text record whose ftop < 0.08 or fbot > 0.92 is a margin candidate. Drop a candidate when `pl_page_number_like` of its text, or when its `pl_norm_key` appears among margin candidates on at least 3 distinct pages and on at least 40 percent of the pages.
- Headings: a kept `dm::DM_PARA` record is a heading candidate when it has at most 3 lines, at most 200 text bytes, and either its size is at least 1.15 × body size, or it is bold, within 10 percent of the body size, has 1 line, at most 120 bytes and does not end with . , ; or :. Outline titles from `pdf::pf_ol_*` win first: a record whose text matches an outline title on the same page (both compared lower-cased with only letters and digits kept; equal, or the title a prefix of a record at most 1.3 times as long) becomes a heading of that outline level. Otherwise sizes of the larger-size candidates, rounded to 0.5, are ranked from largest (level 1) to the fourth (level 4 for that and all smaller), skipping any size whose records hold more than 40 percent of all text; bold body-size candidates get level (number of ranked sizes + 1), at most 6.
- Output: for each page from 0 to `pl_pages` − 1: `dm::dm_page(page + 1, "")`; its records in order: kind 100 is `dm::dm_new_table()`, 101 is `dm::dm_end_table()`, `dm::DM_ROW` adds the row, headings add `dm::DM_HEAD` with their level, other text records add their kind and level (paragraphs level 0), skipping dropped ones; then `pdf::pf_annots(page)`. After the pages call `pdf::pf_fields()`. Add the warning "N of M pages are scanned images without a text layer; OCR is needed to read them" when `pl_scanned` > 0 and store meta "scanned_pages"; add "N characters had no Unicode mapping and were skipped" when `pfont::fo_unmapped` > 0.

`pl_read_pdf(data const byte[:]) bool` reads a whole PDF into `dm`: return false when `pdf::pf_open(data)` fails. Reset `pl_out`, `pl_otxt`, `pl_scanned` and `pfont::fo_reset()`. Call `pdf::pf_info_meta()` and `pdf::pf_outline(true)`. When the trailer has /Encrypt and `dcrypt::dc_on` is false, add no text and return true (the warning is already there). `pl_pages` is the page count, at most 5000 (add the warning "only the first 5000 pages were read" when there are more). For each page: `pcontent::pc_run_page(p)`; when it recorded no glyphs and `pcontent::pc_img` is at least 0.4, count it in `pl_scanned`; then `pl_layout_page(p)`. Finish with `pl_finish()` and return true.
